{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"advisory":[{"agreement":1,"block_threshold":0.5,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Distinguish a genuine `git cat-file` failure from an absent cap blob in `_read_cap_at` (e.g. inspect stderr / known missing-object signal) rather than treating every non-zero returncode as a silent miss.","criteria":["error-handling"],"decision":"advisory","evidence":["src/rebar/metrics/git_metrics.py:208: `if proc.returncode != 0:` `return None`","Docstring asserts 'A missing blob is the ordinary \"not qualified\" case (``git cat-file`` exits non-zero), not a failure' — but the returncode check cannot distinguish a missing blob from any other git failure; stderr is discarded.","The absence-derived verdict ('no cap found') is produced whether the cap is truly absent OR cat-file failed for an unrelated reason, so the check cannot tell 'checked and absent' from 'the check itself failed'."],"finding":"`_read_cap_at` maps ANY non-zero `git cat-file` returncode to `None` ('missing blob / not qualified'), conflating a genuine git error (corrupt object DB, permission failure, unexpected git behavior) with the ordinary absent-cap case. A per-revision cat-file failure on a valid sha is silently treated as 'not qualified', and if it happens for all revisions the metric returns the misleading `unavailable` reason 'no positive-integer module-size cap ... found' rather than surfacing the failure.","id":"0","impact":0.27,"location":"src/rebar/metrics/git_metrics.py:208","norm_id":"n703dcdcb7e5aec97","priority":0.27,"reason":"default-advisory","scenarios":["A repository with a damaged/partial object store where cat-file fails on valid shas reports 'no positive-integer module-size cap found' instead of a git-failur